# ============================================================================
# production-web — what the production server pulls.
#
# This repository is the DEPLOYMENT, not the source. It deliberately carries
# things a normal repository would never commit, and deliberately withholds two
# things a normal deployment folder does contain. Both are explained here so the
# next person does not "tidy" either one.
# ============================================================================


# ── node_modules IS COMMITTED. Do not add it here. ──
#
# `package.json` in this folder says never to run `npm install` on the server,
# and that is the whole design: the Next standalone build already traced exactly
# the packages this application needs, plus socket.io forced in by
# `outputFileTracingIncludes`. Running an install over it would reinstall
# hundreds of packages and can remove what the build traced.
#
# So the ~67 MB of `node_modules` is part of the deployment. That is why the
# server needs no build step and no network access to boot.


# ── SECRETS: never in git, and this repository is PUBLIC ──
#
# `.env.production` holds DB_PASSWORD and a 64-character ENCRYPTION_KEY. Those
# are live production credentials. They are created ONCE on the server, by hand,
# and never travel through git. `.env.production.example` beside this file lists
# the keys that must exist, with no values.
#
# `git pull` does not touch an ignored file, so the server's copy survives every
# deployment.
.env
.env.*
!.env.production.example


# ── RUNTIME DATA: written by the application, owned by the server ──
#
# `public/uploads/` and `public/downloads/` are written at runtime by the upload
# endpoints — claims receipts, payroll payment proofs, certificates, applicant
# documents, helpdesk attachments, news images, branding. They are personal and
# business records, and on a public repository they would be world-readable.
#
# They are also not build output: the development machine's copies are a
# developer's own test data, and production accumulates its own. Committing them
# would push one machine's files at the other.
#
# The DIRECTORIES are kept, because only two of the upload endpoints call
# `mkdirSync`. The rest write straight into an existing folder and fail if it is
# missing, so each one keeps a `.gitkeep`.
public/uploads/*
!public/uploads/*/
public/uploads/*/*
!public/uploads/*/.gitkeep

public/downloads/*
!public/downloads/.gitkeep


# ── PRIVATE RUNTIME DATA: outside public/ on purpose, and still the server's ──
#
# `.uploads-private/` holds files that must NOT be reachable by URL. Next serves
# everything under `public/`, with no session and no permission check, so a
# client's signed handover form, an employee's IC copy and a claim receipt go
# here instead and are read only through an endpoint that checks the session and
# the owning row.
#
# HR receipts moved here from `public/uploads/claims` and
# `public/uploads/expenses`, where they were readable by anyone who knew the
# path — and the claim filenames carried no random part at all, so one seen file
# let its siblings be guessed. `src/lib/hr-receipts.ts` records the measurement.
#
# Withheld for both of the reasons the public upload folders are. They are
# PERSONAL records — medical bills, invoices, IC and passport scans — and this
# repository is public. They are also runtime data: the development machine's
# copies are a developer's test files, and production accumulates its own.
#
# The DIRECTORIES are kept, because the package creates them and the server
# needs the path to exist before the first upload rather than after a failed one.
.uploads-private/*
!.uploads-private/*/
.uploads-private/*/*
!.uploads-private/*/.gitkeep


# ── The pre-validation staging directory is never deployment content ──
#
# `.uploads-tmp/` holds an upload for the few milliseconds between arriving and
# being accepted. Unlike `.uploads-private/` it is disposable and may be emptied
# at will, so nothing in it belongs in git — not even a `.gitkeep`, because
# `operations-upload.ts` creates it with `mkdir -p` on every parse.
.uploads-tmp/


# ── Build cache: 4.7 GB, and never needed to run ──
#
# The assembly step excludes it with `robocopy /XD cache`, so it should never
# appear here. This is the second line of defence.
.next/cache/


# ── Local noise ──
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
